1
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:05,360
你好

2
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:07,360
Is it right?

3
00:00:07,361 --> 00:00:19,600
Thank you so much for having me and I'm Gyuri Kim, an iOS developer at Babitalk, a beauty and cosmetic care review platform.

4
00:00:19,601 --> 00:00:28,931
We run two apps now, one for Korea and a global one in Japan with over 10 million downloads.

5
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:40,480
So today, instead of a bunch of little tricks of AI, I want to walk you through the change in thinking that finally got me out of the AI's way.

6
00:00:43,119 --> 00:00:50,880
How do you refactor a code base so large that no single person fully understands it anymore?

7
00:00:50,881 --> 00:00:55,679
And years of decisions many by people long gone.

8
00:00:55,680 --> 00:01:05,119
And before I tell you what I tried, I have to be precise about what makes a code base like this hard for an AI.

9
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:15,439
And in this presentation, when I say "legacy", it means any code from the pre-AI area.

10
00:01:16,239 --> 00:01:22,159
This isn't only about the bad code, like the code we commonly say spaghetti code.

11
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:31,679
Rather I'd like to say even elegant and beautiful code can be legacy to an AI if its context lives in someone's head.

12
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:42,479
In other words, if understanding any unfamiliar code requires you to dig through documents like messages or find stakeholders.

13
00:01:43,016 --> 00:01:48,479
Then that code base can be called as legacy for an AI.

14
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:54,079
Here's the whole app, folder by folder.

15
00:01:54,080 --> 00:02:00,719
Most of it is clean, organized into modern modules and easy to work in.

16
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:03,759
But there's one folder.

17
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:06,560
The one nobody opens.

18
00:02:06,561 --> 00:02:12,189
Inside it, hundreds of huge files built over years.

19
00:02:12,714 --> 00:02:16,904
And the architecture is all mixed together with no clear rules.

20
00:02:16,958 --> 00:02:25,201
And the people who wrote it they were moved on and I still remember the first time I opened one of those files.

21
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:28,960
I scrolled for what felt like forever.

22
00:02:28,961 --> 00:02:36,319
And I honestly couldn't tell what it did or where one thing ended and the next began.

23
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:42,319
My first reaction was like this close the tab and hope someone else would deal with it.

24
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:51,840
But it wasn't going anymore anywhere and neither was I until I did.

25
00:02:51,841 --> 00:02:58,719
So it's not just me open any file in there and you can't tell what it does or why.

26
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:02,079
And even if you change one line, nothing tells you

27
00:03:04,159 --> 00:03:06,719
Nothing tells you what it happens.

28
00:03:11,039 --> 00:03:12,879
How much is "a lot"?

29
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:14,080
I mapped it.

30
00:03:14,081 --> 00:03:22,479
It's about six thousand types, twenty-two thousand dependencies across dozens of screens.

31
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:25,600
And you don't do this by hand forever.

32
00:03:25,601 --> 00:03:31,200
Either there's a method on AI can drive or there isn't.

33
00:03:31,201 --> 00:03:33,439
I didn't boil the ocean.

34
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:40,000
Inside the folder, I took one screen. Let's say feature A, and the logic behind it.

35
00:03:40,001 --> 00:03:44,800
Get it right, learn the method and then judge honestly whether it scales.

36
00:03:47,599 --> 00:03:51,040
Let me walk you through the method six steps.

37
00:03:51,041 --> 00:03:58,080
First, map the dependencies so I can see what this one screen actually touches.

38
00:03:58,081 --> 00:04:03,919
And second step, it was split it into the modules along that seams.

39
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:07,356
And third pull the business logic out ,out of the view.

40
00:04:07,893 --> 00:04:15,439
And the fourth make the logic testable and fifth rebuild the view in SwiftUI from UIkit.

41
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:20,480
And last prove the behavior did not change.

42
00:04:20,481 --> 00:04:24,639
I ran AI on every single one of these steps.

43
00:04:26,399 --> 00:04:29,200
But two steps were generally fast.

44
00:04:29,201 --> 00:04:38,000
The AI mapped, the dependency grab in one pass and turned the design into a first pass SwiftUI view.

45
00:04:38,001 --> 00:04:40,959
The static layout quickly.

46
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:51,120
Brand new screen will at be most of the way there.

47
00:04:51,121 --> 00:04:52,720
But it wasn't new.

48
00:04:52,721 --> 00:04:58,720
Behind that one simple screen, correct means very specific things learned over years.

49
00:04:58,959 --> 00:05:04,877
What happens when the AI API fails and which edge cases renders which view.

50
00:05:04,878 --> 00:05:10,377
And which analytics every event fires with which parameters

51
00:05:10,749 --> 00:05:14,590
None in the design file or in that controller.

52
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:22,720
So my rewrite had to reproduce everyone and nothing could check that I had expect me.

53
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:32,400
So I tuned every step, sharper prompts and faster models, cleaner handoffs.

54
00:05:32,401 --> 00:05:38,400
Each step got faster remarkably, and the whole thing barely moved.

55
00:05:38,401 --> 00:05:47,600
The time was never inside the steps, it was between them and between that steps was me.

56
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:54,549
The AI carried the mechanical ones mapping dependencies.

57
00:05:54,877 --> 00:06:03,439
Split it into modules, generating the first-pass SwiftUI view fast mostly hands up.

58
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:08,720
But the steps that decided whether any of it actually works stays mine.

59
00:06:08,750 --> 00:06:11,003
Pulling the real behavior out of the view.

60
00:06:11,724 --> 00:06:17,360
And step four making it testable, the real work.

61
00:06:17,361 --> 00:06:23,120
In a screen that tangled there was a no seam to grab one to.

62
00:06:23,121 --> 00:06:25,759
So I had to manufacture one.

63
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:28,240
That's just that the AI couldn't just hand me.

64
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:36,800
And a lot stuff too was rebuilding SwiftUI and prove unchanged.

65
00:06:36,801 --> 00:06:41,120
I had to generate the first-pass view and then I have to still.

66
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:54,000
Then I have to still check the manual steps if there is anything changed badly.

67
00:06:55,519 --> 00:07:02,240
So this is the moment it clicked ,I set out to refactor a screen.

68
00:07:02,241 --> 00:07:06,800
What I was actually doing was moving code across the line from pre-AI

69
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:18,320
Where the things an AI needs to work live outside the code to AI native where they live inside it.

70
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:20,879
And the old code wasn't wrong.

71
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:29,040
It was built when writing code was so expensive and a human held all of it in their head.

72
00:07:29,041 --> 00:07:31,040
The ground moved under it

73
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:42,160
For months, a couple of months, my instinct was to jump in and fix things myself one step at a time.

74
00:07:42,161 --> 00:07:51,120
And it that was the mistake and the real change wasn't a sharper prompt or a faster model those come later.

75
00:07:51,121 --> 00:07:52,880
It was my attitude.

76
00:07:52,881 --> 00:08:03,120
I had to stop standing in the middle of every step and pullback to set things up so the AI could work freely without me.

77
00:08:03,121 --> 00:08:11,280
And to do that, I had to hand it the two things I had to supply by hand alone.

78
00:08:11,680 --> 00:08:22,800
They finally had names, context, context to what the tangled code actually means and criterion way to know it still works.

79
00:08:22,801 --> 00:08:31,439
Give the AI those two and it doesn't need me in the middle anymore.

80
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:41,360
The first insight I got is the AI was never short on capability, it was short on context working half blind.

81
00:08:41,361 --> 00:08:50,422
So before I optimized a single step, I changed the project itself so the AI could understand what it was working inside.

82
00:08:52,480 --> 00:08:56,000
So let me name what I was actually doing.

83
00:08:56,399 --> 00:08:59,920
I call it reverse AI engineering.

84
00:08:59,921 --> 00:09:02,800
And it's not using AI on my code.

85
00:09:03,039 --> 00:09:08,480
It's converting pre-AI code into the code an AI can own.

86
00:09:08,481 --> 00:09:11,600
Legacy code has lost its context.

87
00:09:11,601 --> 00:09:17,519
The why, the reason lives in people's heads, not in the code.

88
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:19,920
It just spread out everywhere.

89
00:09:19,921 --> 00:09:36,400
So to put it back, I had the AI read the old scattered code and write down what it actually does, the rules and the edge cases and all end-features.

90
00:09:36,401 --> 00:09:42,320
The events also included in the spec and it fires.

91
00:09:42,639 --> 00:09:48,397
Spare a real machine readable document and that now lives in the repository

92
00:09:48,645 --> 00:09:58,159
And the AI reverse engineered the context nobody had written down and honestly better than I could from memory.

93
00:09:58,160 --> 00:10:03,760
And that's the conversion taking what the AI needs to work.

94
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:08,000
This is the second insight.

95
00:10:08,001 --> 00:10:15,840
A step can run without me and the moment a machine can judge "done" for it.

96
00:10:15,841 --> 00:10:24,320
So the goal became simple and give every step a check machine can run a criterion.

97
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,960
Here's a simple example you can understand.

98
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:48,000
In an AI build loop, you hit errors after a build and you might copy and paste the error messages into the ai and it fixes them you build against the new errors and send those back again.

99
00:10:48,001 --> 00:11:03,680
And if you want to run the loop without checking every step yourself, you might or can prompt something like fix any compiler errors until the build succeed.

100
00:11:03,681 --> 00:11:07,440
So why they work easily?

101
00:11:07,441 --> 00:11:11,120
Because that build loop runs without a human.

102
00:11:11,121 --> 00:11:24,399
It has two things a clear trigger and a compiler error and a clear criterion the build passes. When a machine can judge done I do not need to sit in the middle.

103
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:35,120
Thats the shape I went looking for on every step, something to trigger it and a criterion and machine can judge to call it done.

104
00:11:35,121 --> 00:11:37,200
Be honest about the limit.

105
00:11:37,519 --> 00:11:49,600
The new screen has a criterion now test going forward, and the one I couldn't manufacture. Does it behave identically to the old screen ?

106
00:11:49,601 --> 00:12:03,039
and the API path edge cases, analytics. No test could run that because the old code couldn't be tested at all, so I checked by hand at the first moment.

107
00:12:03,040 --> 00:12:11,440
And that's one spot is where I stayed at the bottleneck, just like I said before.

108
00:12:11,441 --> 00:12:14,080
And here's why I changed on the pilot.

109
00:12:14,081 --> 00:12:24,720
Once context was in a criterion queue run, the loop that used to bounce back to me and every hand of ran on its own.

110
00:12:24,721 --> 00:12:28,880
So this isn't about removing the human, it's about moving the human.

111
00:12:29,759 --> 00:12:44,879
So what still reaches me is the judgment only a person should make the ambiguous case and the real risk and the moment the AI is confidently wrong and someone has to catch it.

112
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:47,200
Same person but better place.

113
00:12:48,720 --> 00:12:51,440
Here's where this stands.

114
00:12:51,441 --> 00:13:01,759
It started with one pilot screen and now I'm taking it across the application reason by reason and feature by feature and folder by folder.

115
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:15,280
So the method holds up gets context into the code and give each steps a criterion a machine can check and the loop runs on its own.

116
00:13:15,281 --> 00:13:21,840
The one piece I still handle myself is the final check and does the

117
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:38,800
And does the new screen behave exactly like the old one and I know how to fold the event that into the loop, reuse the spec documents and the AI reverse generates as the oracle.

118
00:13:38,801 --> 00:13:42,159
Same context but doing double duty.

119
00:13:44,320 --> 00:14:02,799
So if you or AI work flies on small tasks but stall on a big ones old ones, a capable model, the right one per test still matters a lot but it's the most powerful level you have.

120
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:11,600
But if it's still inefficient you think and look at where in the pipeline you keep stepping in.

121
00:14:11,601 --> 00:14:15,680
For me it came down to two words.

122
00:14:15,681 --> 00:14:20,720
One is context to put the scatter "why" back into the repository.

123
00:14:20,721 --> 00:14:23,279
And the second one is criterion.

124
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:29,279
The standard give each step a check a machine can run.

125
00:14:29,280 --> 00:14:35,040
Wherever one is missing, a human feels it and becomes the bottleneck.

126
00:14:38,800 --> 00:14:48,399
And you might be thinking, isn't it just applying a test harness or harness engineering and give some context like that?

127
00:14:48,400 --> 00:14:53,998
But yeah it's fair and but here's what I want to leave you with.

128
00:14:53,999 --> 00:15:11,039
The judgment we've always had as engineers choosing the architecture and the design pattern that actually fits the problem, not just following the trend that didn't disappear, but it just moved.

129
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:18,879
Now it points at the new question, how do I set things up so the AI works well?

130
00:15:18,880 --> 00:15:22,880
How do I break the pipeline into steps?

131
00:15:22,881 --> 00:15:28,560
And where exactly I still the one who has to step in.

132
00:15:28,561 --> 00:15:49,279
So it's before as an legacy developer myself. I just build the architecture in the code base and only in point of the code, but now as a AI native developer you can build the architecture in point of AI view.

133
00:15:49,280 --> 00:15:58,000
So that's not the similar job, but it's the same instinct aimed at a new target.

134
00:15:58,001 --> 00:16:02,800
So I'd like to say our jobs are safe probably.

135
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:09,440
And yeah that's my story and thank you for listening my presentation.

136
00:16:09,441 --> 00:16:10,240
Thank you.

137
00:16:11,440 --> 00:16:11,680
(APPLAUSE)
